{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"for skill in memory-log scientific-evidence validate-paper write-paper-standalone generate-concept-figure; do printf '\\\\n=== %s ===\\\\n' \\\"\"'$skill\"; cat \".codex/skills/$skill/SKILL.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"\n=== memory-log ===\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n=== scientific-evidence ===\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n\n=== validate-paper ===\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n\n=== write-paper-standalone ===\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n\n=== generate-concept-figure ===\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat paper_strategy.md; head -100 paper.tex; tail -55 paper.log; ls paper_diagnostics; command -v pdflatex; command -v apply_patch; find /tmp /opt -name apply_patch -type f 2>/dev/null'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat paper_strategy.md; head -100 paper.tex; tail -55 paper.log; ls paper_diagnostics; command -v pdflatex; command -v apply_patch; find /tmp /opt -name apply_patch -type f 2>/dev/null'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n{\n  \"status\": \"no_gaps\",\n  \"summary\": \"The draft directly cites the relevant ranking, influence/removal, manipulation, dataset, and resampling literatures, while its method and quantitative findings are artifact-grounded, so no targeted post-draft retrieval is justified.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status: no_gaps**\n\nThe draft directly cites the relevant ranking, influence/removal, manipulation, dataset, and resampling literatures, while its method and quantitative findings are artifact-grounded, so no targeted post-draft retrieval is justified.\n\n## Grounded Review\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary was used only as qualitative context; current manuscript and evidence artifacts determine the decision. All 25 distinct manuscript citation keys have matching bibliography and provenance records. Provenance records distinguish bibliographic-only consultation from abstracts and dataset documentation; this review does not imply full-text verification.\n\n## Coverage Assessment\n\n- **Related Work — Pairwise ranking:** Direct citations cover Bradley–Terry foundations, generalized estimation, tie models, bias reduction, permutation models, Rank Centrality, counting-based recovery, and the inversion metric. No mentioned comparator family is left without a direct citation.\n- **Related Work — Influence and sample removal:** Hampel1974, Cook1977, Koh2017, Giordano2019, and Broderick2020 directly support local sensitivity, observation diagnostics, weighted-refit approximations, and finite-sample deletion positioning. The draft explicitly avoids exhaustive-search and approximation-bound claims.\n- **Related Work — Evaluation and conditional uncertainty:** Chiang2024 and NBAdata establish dataset context; Min2025 supports the distinction from vote rigging. Efron1979, Davison1997, Rubin1981, Field2007, and Owen2007 cover bootstrap, alternative weighting, and clustered/crossed dependence context. The conditional diagnostic is not presented as validated population or full-procedure uncertainty.\n- **Introduction and Method:** The introduction makes no literature-wide superiority or novelty-priority claim. Its uncited general framing can already draw on the directly cited Related Work literature if citation placement is later adjusted; this does not require retrieval. Frozen prefixes, shortlist selection, baseline definitions, independent refits, and the retained-mean correction describe the supplied implementation or its algebra, rather than unsupported external benchmark facts.\n- **Experimental Setup, Results, and Discussion and Limitations:** Quantitative comparisons and protocol details are grounded in `paper_data.json` and the source locators recorded in `claims_grounding.json`. Missing controlled ablations, full reselection uncertainty, and raw-data reconstruction are explicitly disclosed evidence limitations, not citation gaps.\n\n## Decision\n\n- Missing literatures: none identified that justify retrieval for the current narrative.\n- Rewrite targets: none.\n- Failure reason: none.\n\nThe strategy predates the current bibliography: its warning about missing exact reference identities does not override their presence and direct use in the completed draft. Do not broaden the study to unrestricted attacks, new benchmark families, or adaptive uncertainty merely to expand citation coverage.\n\nNo references were retrieved. `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` were not modified.\n# Paper strategy\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone`. **Writing skill:** `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** Exact-Refit Audits of Pairwise Ranking Sensitivity\n\n## Central question\n\nHow do small, influence-ordered deletions affect regularized Bradley–Terry rankings, and how does their disruption compare with random and high-loss deletion across crowd preferences and historical sports outcomes?\n\n## Central thesis\n\nA frozen-prefix audit with bounded exact-refit contender selection yields greater aggregate rank disruption than the measured deletion baselines on the final partitions, but its advantage depends on dataset and deletion budget, with fixed-mask group resampling favoring high-loss deletion in Arena and the studied selector in NBA.\n\n## Why this story\n\nThe strongest evidence is the direct final comparison on two real-data settings and its conditional uncertainty analysis, not an optimization success story. Use one common sensitivity question with condition-specific evidence blocks, not a causal test of evaluation design. The inspected implementation supports the mechanism description; evaluator refits support measured effectiveness. Search configurations are descriptive supporting material, not independently replicated final ablations. The paper is viable as a bounded empirical audit without universal superiority or novelty-priority claims.\n\n## Framing decision\n\nThis is fresh framing, not an edit pass: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or prior manuscript are staged. The status means recentering the source-task brief, not editing an unseen paper. Compare three alternatives: preserving the proposed dependence-aware, multi-judge frontier story is unsupported; broadening to a universal cross-design robustness method overstates unmatched evidence; centering the shared frozen-prefix row-deletion audit and heterogeneous measured limits is supported. Method detail and figure density are not reasons to preserve an unsupported story.\n\nPrior-story status: **recentered** (source-brief recentering; no prior manuscript was staged).\n\n## Grounded input impact\n\n- Read final_evaluation independently: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, final.qualification.correct=true. There is no downstream_study or study.* grounding. Qualification is not statistical superiority or generalization; missing additional gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n\n- results/best_program_manifest.json identifies the six-contender exact-refit configuration. Its inspected source supports the named method family; final provenance records a program-tree digest, but an independent reconstruction of that digest is not claimed.\n\n- Final episodes cover Arena and NBA, not the proposed MT-Bench expert/GPT-4 comparisons. Provenance groups support partitioning and conditional resampling, not whole-group deletion or design labels available to the selector.\n\n- Final dataset/budget contrasts and unique fixed-mask bootstrap records shift the thesis away from a general improvement claim. Reversed-row cases are controls, not extra datasets; repeated bootstrap exports are not new resamples.\n\n- Clean-Hessian selection and retained-curvature expansion have descriptive search evidence only. No valid grounded metric for the original starter comparator supports retaining its quantitative comparison.\n\n- References and provenance include irrelevant retrieval matches and do not resolve all exact planned identities. The bibliography is not evidence of a completed literature review or established novelty.\n\n## Results focus\n\n- Lead with final dataset-by-budget normalized inversion fractions for the studied selector, high-loss deletion, and random deletion. Use unpermuted cases for performance summaries and reversed-row cases only for invariance checks.\n\n- Secondary final aggregate table: studied selector 0.011722185737596697, high-loss deletion 0.009340217174121283, random deletion 0.003902759168170127; paired gains over high-loss and random deletion 0.002381968563475412 and 0.00781942656942657. Sources: final.metric.combined_score, final.metric.disagreement_baseline_score, final.metric.random_baseline_score, final.metric.paired_gain_vs_disagreement, final.metric.paired_gain_vs_random.\n\n- Display every unique stored conditional group-bootstrap gain. Each dataset has 32 records, repeated identically across five episode exports, not 160 independent records. All stored high-loss-relative gains are negative in Arena and positive in NBA; Arena random-relative gains include negative observations. Source: final.evidence.episodes.\n\n- Separate seed-resampling intervals from group-weight resampling. The high-loss-relative seed interval has equal endpoints because that contrast is deterministic across recorded seeds; it is not certainty about data variation or general superiority.\n\n- Use normalized original-leader rank drop 0.03305066318764949 only as a secondary aggregate for the studied selector, grounded in final.metric.mean_leader_rank_drop. Per-budget curves are normalized inversions, not leader drops or minimal reversal budgets.\n\n- A small descriptive search table may show raw scores only: clean-Hessian-only selection 0.013240276724865765 (result.top_program_03), bounded exact refits 0.01595532053579999 (result.top_program_01), retained-curvature expansion 0.01595532053579999 (result.top_program_02). Keep this separate from final results; no campaign winner, derived search-delta, or scored/unscored count claims without study.* grounding.\n\n## Method groups\n\n- **Frozen-prefix bounded exact-refit selector** (proposed): The studied six-contender configuration computes clean influence orders, selects using retained-data refits, and returns frozen nested prefixes for independent evaluation.\n\n- **Uniform random row deletion** (baseline): Measured at identical floored row budgets; five seeds reflect deletion randomness, not independent dataset draws.\n\n- **High-loss row deletion** (baseline): Strong measured comparator ordered by clean per-row Bradley–Terry negative log-likelihood; it is not disagreement between judges. Its Arena advantage is essential.\n\n- **Unmodified-data and reversed-row controls** (supporting): Numerical zero-change and row-order invariance checks, not reader-facing performance rates or additional replications.\n\n- **Clean-Hessian-only contender selection** (ablation): Descriptive search-configuration comparison for lookahead, explicitly not a replicated final ablation or causal mechanism attribution.\n\n- **Retained-curvature shortlist expansion** (ablation): Supporting negative configuration comparison: expanded proxy/exact pools tie the recorded search aggregate. Do not infer identical behavior, equivalence, or efficiency.\n\n## Figure plan and final-size geometry\n\nInspected `paper.sty`, the ICLR skill template, and `VENUE.md`: a single 5.5-inch-wide text column and 9-inch text height. Column width equals full text width; do not apply a two-column assumption. Figures are ordinary text-width figures.\n\n### audit_overview — required\n\nGive immediate glance value for the common sensitivity object, method, and conditioning boundary.\n\n- Generator/type: `generate-concept-figure` / `diagram`.\n\n- Encoding: group by audit_component; split by two dataset badges feeding one common audit; metric: none; conceptual mechanism only.\n\n- Content: Six blocks: observed comparisons (Arena crowd preferences / NBA outcomes); regularized Bradley–Terry fit; frozen influence prefixes; bounded exact-refit contender selection; deletion and independent refits; dataset/budget contrasts. Attach a short fixed-mask resampling/no-reselection note to the last block. Do not depict grouped deletion, architecture changes, adaptive row reordering, or causal design effects.\n\n- Readability budget: 1 panels; at most 1 panels per row; 6 categories and 3 series per panel; labels at least 9 pt. No progression view.\n\n- Placement: Page 1 or 2, before the detailed method, using an ordinary single-column figure. Width: 0.98 of text width (full_width).\n\n- Footprint: About 0.65 page including caption; artwork about 2.6 inches tall.\n\n- Layout justification: Inspected paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height. At 0.98 text width, about 5.39 inches, a two-row/three-block arrangement avoids tiny text. Half-width placement would obscure frozen-prefix versus refit distinctions.\n\n- Grounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`. Supporting protocol/implementation: `results/ideas/02_exact_refit_contender_screening/program_1/src/program.py`, `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n\n### dataset_budget_comparison — required\n\nExpose dataset and budget contrasts hidden by aggregation.\n\n- Generator/type: `matplotlib` / `scatter`, with grouped-dot layout.\n\n- Encoding: group by deletion_rule; split by dataset; metric: normalized pairwise inversion fraction relative to clean fitted ranking.\n\n- Content: Three nominal removed-row fractions: 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, with floored counts stated in the caption. Plot deterministic studied/high-loss markers without manufactured uncertainty bars; show five random-deletion seed values as faint same-color points and their mean. Do not count reversed-row controls as replicates. Shared y scale; larger means greater disruption, not better ranking accuracy. Budget is an experimental condition, not search progression.\n\n- Readability budget: 2 panels; at most 2 panels per row; 3 categories and 3 series per panel; labels at least 9 pt. No progression view.\n\n- Placement: Main results, approximately page 5 or 6. Width: 1.0 of text width (full_width).\n\n- Footprint: About 0.70 page including caption; artwork about 3.0 inches tall.\n\n- Layout justification: Use all 5.5 inches for two side-by-side panels, each with three budgets and three methods, common axes and one shared legend. Do not compress the entire figure to half-text width or rescue long labels by shrinking them.\n\n- Grounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`. Supporting protocol/implementation: `task/evaluate_final.py:FRACTIONS`, `task/evaluate_final.py:disruption`.\n\n### conditional_resampling_contrasts — required\n\nKeep dependence-sensitive conditional uncertainty and dataset-specific comparator ordering visible in the main paper.\n\n- Generator/type: `matplotlib` / `scatter`, with strip-plot layout.\n\n- Encoding: group by dataset; split by high-loss deletion and random deletion comparators; metric: fixed-mask bootstrap difference in mean normalized inversions: studied selector minus comparator.\n\n- Content: Extract one unique conditional_group_bootstrap block and plot its 32 stored gains per dataset per comparator against zero. Label fixed-mask group-weight resampling. Do not pool duplicated exports, call this full-procedure uncertainty, claim population significance, or infer causal evaluation-design effects.\n\n- Readability budget: 2 panels; at most 2 panels per row; 2 categories and 1 series per panel; labels at least 9 pt. No progression view.\n\n- Placement: Immediately after Figure 2, approximately page 6 or 7; never supplementary-only. Width: 1.0 of text width (full_width).\n\n- Footprint: About 0.65 page including caption; artwork about 2.7 inches tall.\n\n- Layout justification: Two panels across the full 5.5-inch text block leave room for 32 observations per dataset, explicit comparator titles, zero reference, and labels at least 9 pt. Use reproducible display jitter, not more statistical samples.\n\n- Grounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`. Supporting protocol/implementation: `task/evaluate_final.py:final_uncertainty`.\n\n## Supplementary plan\nProvide implementation, provenance, numerical-control, and uncertainty depth needed to reproduce and audit the narrow claim, without moving the decisive contrasts or limitations out of the main paper.\n\n### Retained-mean objective and frozen-prefix selection\nExpand centered ridge Bradley–Terry, half-win ties, clean contrast/influence solves, retained-mean displacement correction, deterministic tie rules, six-contender selection, pair-statistic compression, warm starts/cache, convergence tolerances, incomplete-refit fallback, and selector/evaluation metric mismatch. Inspected program.py, IMPLEMENTATION.md, and evaluator explain the mechanism, not unreported internal shortlist decisions.\n\nGrounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n\n### Data partitioning and reproducibility record\nRecord pinned Arena/NBA sources, content-group split, largest connected components, NBA copy filtering and historical team-code limitations, source/payload hashes, declared seeds, CPU dependencies and staged-resource commands from inspected manifests/code. Distinguish protocol metadata from measured findings. A complete raw-to-partition builder and all original raw archives are not staged; do not claim otherwise.\n\nGrounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`.\n\n### Numerical and row-order checks\nExplain residual tolerances, no-deletion zero disruption, invalid constant-certificate rejection, reversed-row invariance, and unverified candidate-authored diagnostics. Preserve the exact final qualification outcome without inferring undocumented gate causes or generalization.\n\nGrounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, `final.qualification.correct`.\n\n### Descriptive search configurations\nArchive raw scores and inspected implementations for clean-Hessian selection, bounded exact refits and retained-curvature expansion. State missing final controlled ablations and selection-adjusted uncertainty. A tied aggregate is not identical behavior or an overhead measurement. Do not report campaign counts, unscored losses, derived search deltas, or original-starter performance without study.* sources.\n\nGrounding IDs: `result.top_program_01`, `result.top_program_02`, `result.top_program_03`.\n\n### Conditional uncertainty protocol and stored records\nGive the complete unique group-weight records and bootstrap algorithm; masks are fixed, weighted removed mass varies, and selection is not rerun. Contrast this with aggregate seed-resampling intervals and their deterministic degeneracy. The main text retains opposite high-loss-relative signs and the conditioning limitations.\n\nGrounding IDs: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_low`, `final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_high`, `final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_low`, `final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_high`.\n\n## Required process disclosure\nThe five source-backed supplementary entries above describe scientific evidence. Separately, include the venue-mandated **LLM Usage** subsection in Supplementary Information as process disclosure, not as an additional result-grounded entry.\n\nDisclose the roles documented in supplied agent-run logs and artifacts: research ideation, candidate-code generation, reference-query planning, and manuscript assistance. Distinguish these from the numerical evaluator. Do not invent provider/model identities, human oversight or paid-API experiments; this is process provenance, not a numerical result.\n\nDocumented agent-run artifacts support this disclosure; no numerical grounding ID is assigned to agent-process roles.\n\n## Writer contract: body budget, output, and limitations\n\n1. Scope: this strategy writes neither paper.tex nor claims_grounding.json, drafts no final paper, renders no figures, runs no new experiment, and invokes no final-paper validation. Dotted JSON fields, internal selection terms and hashes in this memo are private-only source maps, not visible manuscript labels. Memory is qualitative planning context only.\n\n2. Venue/layout: use VENUE.md and the supplied ICLR 2026 style. The inspected paper.sty has textwidth=5.5 inches and textheight=9 inches, single-column: column width equals full text width. All three figures use this text block, not a two-column figure* layout. Keep margins and typography unchanged.\n\n3. Body budget, approximately 8.5 pages including figures/tables but excluding references: title/abstract 0.5; introduction 1.0; related work 0.6; method 1.6; data/metrics/uncertainty protocol 1.1; results 2.6; discussion/limitations 0.8; conclusion 0.3. These are planned page equivalents, not measured pagination. Venue requires 8–9 body pages; an eight-page body must end at least 75% full. Spend space on scientific explanation, not padding or unsupported experiments.\n\n4. Thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work; Frozen-Prefix Ranking Audits; Data, Metrics, and Uncertainty; Dataset- and Budget-Dependent Disruption; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Use three figures plus two compact tables: final aggregate metrics with seed-interval interpretation, and descriptive search configurations. Keep search context brief and distinct from final evidence.\n\n5. Protocol from inspected code: mean negative log-likelihood plus lambda=0.001 ridge, centered scores, ties as half wins, nominal deletion fractions 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, floored row counts. For removed prefix S of size k among n rows, the screening displacement is H_clean inverse applied to (sum of removed row gradients + k*lambda*theta_clean), divided by n-k. Distinguish that approximation from converged retained-data fits; exact means residual-controlled numerical refitting, not symbolic exactness.\n\n6. Definitions: normalized inversion fraction divides discordant participant pairs by all participant pairs. Normalized original-leader rank drop divides its downward position change by participant count minus one. Per-budget curves are inversions, not leader drops. The selector uses leader-drop objectives, which need not optimize the reported inversion statistic. Larger disruption does not imply ranking inaccuracy or better models.\n\n7. Comparison scope: row fraction normalizes intervention size, not effective sample size, participant exposure, graph geometry, leverage or uncertainty. Two unmatched designs do not identify a causal design effect. NBA historical team codes/eras are heterogeneous. Fit-specific ranking sensitivity is not evidence about present-day platform rankings or participant ability across eras.\n\n8. Uncertainty: the aggregate high-loss-relative seed interval is [0.002381968563475412, 0.002381968563475412]; the random-relative interval is [0.007377907506332164, 0.008168732424939618]. Source each endpoint with its final.metric.paired_gain_*_ci_* ID. Explain seed-resampling construction in the main table caption. Repeated deterministic contrasts do not estimate data uncertainty.\n\n9. Conditional group resampling holds original masks fixed, refits weighted data, and does not rerun contender selection or either baseline order. Removed weighted mass is not fixed by nominal row fraction. There are 32 unique stored records per dataset, repeated across exported episodes; never pool these into 160 independent records. No full-procedure confidence or population-wide significance claim.\n\n10. No matched-support experiment, whole-group deletion frontier, expert/judge comparison, adaptive order refinement, ridge-sensitivity study, runtime ablation or global minimum-deletion certificate was measured. Provenance groups support split/resampling only; the selector receives anonymous rows. Do not turn implementation intent into empirical findings.\n\n11. Qualification: current authoritative final sources report completed and qualified. This does not establish generalization or universal superiority. If later authoritative evidence reports failed qualification, disclose and preserve it through writing, review, audit and revision; distinguish statistical/performance causes from validity/adaptivity causes only when identified by evaluator diagnostics. Missing causes stay unknown.\n\n12. Source map: final.evidence.episodes supports per-case data and conditional resampling; final.metric.* supports aggregate metrics; final.qualification.* alone grounds qualification. No downstream_study or study.* sources exist, so omit campaign selection advantages, derived search-score differences, attempt counts and original-starter quantitative comparison. Use result.top_program_* only for clearly labeled descriptive raw search scores. Unscored performance is missing, not a loss.\n\n13. Positioning: claim an auditable fitted-ranking protocol, measured two-setting budget contrasts and conditional uncertainty with limitations. Do not claim new Bradley–Terry estimation, novelty priority, universally effective attacks, or causal effects of evaluation design. The two exact-refit configurations share a search aggregate, not proven equivalence; no comparative CPU-cost claim is available.\n\n14. References: staged retrieval has irrelevant matches, duplicate Bradley–Terry records, and lacks several exact planned influence-function, finite-sample robustness, Arena, vote-rigging and NBA provenance identities. Later verify exact allowed identities and actually consult their content; use synchronized BibTeX/provenance keys only. Existing matches are not citation permission. No broad leaderboard searches, citation traversal, guessing of the prohibited work, or citations to unconsulted material. This strategy makes no network requests.\n\n15. Figure outputs: quantitative plots require reproducible source, vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI or higher. LaTeX must reference PDF whenever it exists. Prefer editable draw.io plus PDF/PNG for the concept overview; if raster-only, require at least 2400 px width for these full-width figures, or 1200 px for any later justified column-scale raster. Regenerate rather than upscale. Follow concept-figure orchestration later; do not render during strategy generation.\n\n16. Readability: labels should render at 9–10 pt or larger. Method colors stay consistent within the comparison; dataset colors in the uncertainty figure have an explicitly different labeled role. A zero reference is not another method. No thresholded success-rate plot is warranted. Simplify or regenerate dense figures rather than shrinking.\n\n17. Supplement: substantive material already exists. After references use a clear page, appendix mode, and Supplementary Information in the same paper source; include a separate LLM Usage subsection. Authoritative posthoc rankings arrive separately; route appendix-ranked analyses there without guessing rankings now or moving central evidence out of the main text.\n\n18. Anonymity: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying links and internal URLs. Remove template final-copy/author scaffolding during drafting while preserving its required watermark. Include the separate LLM Usage disclosure; do not invent a mandatory checklist not supplied for this venue.\n\n19. Reproducibility: inspected sources include the candidate, evaluator, normalized resources/manifests and numpy==2.2.6, scipy==1.15.3 requirements. A complete raw-to-partition builder and all raw originals are not staged. Inventory actual available artifacts/commands and distinguish staged-resource reruns from raw-source reconstruction. Do not claim the broader reproduction package is already complete.\n\n## Forbidden headings\n**Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Valid Result**, **Timeline of Improvements**, **Experiment A**, **Experiment B**, **From Failure to Success**, **Winning Configuration**.\n\n## Forbidden phrases\n**we then tried**, **first valid result**, **subsequent improvements**, **next-best**, **later variants**, **search winner**, **universally superior**, **universally more robust**, **state of the art**, **globally minimal deletion budget**, **causal effect of evaluation design**, **full-procedure bootstrap**, **five independent datasets**, **160 independent bootstrap replicates**, **correctness rate**, **correct and eligible**, **eligible**, **combined_score**, **rubric**, **worker_01**, **worker_02**.\n\nThese lists prohibit chronology framing, unsupported claims, and pipeline-native visible labels; source identifiers elsewhere in this strategy are private-only grounding, not exceptions.\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{xcolor,xfp,draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,xurl}\n\\newcommand{\\sigmoid}{\\operatorname{sigmoid}}\n\\newcommand{\\Exact}{\\textsc{Exact-refit}}\n\\title{Exact-Refit Audits of Pairwise Ranking Sensitivity}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPairwise-comparison leaderboards compress observed outcomes into an ordered list, but the stability of that list depends on which observations remain. We study a constrained deletion audit for regularized Bradley--Terry rankings: clean-fit influence values define nested deletion prefixes, a bounded shortlist of contenders is compared by retained-data refits, and an independent solver measures the resulting ranking changes. Evaluations cover crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences and historical basketball outcomes at three small deletion fractions. Averaged over settings and budgets, the studied selector inverts 1.172\\% of participant pairs, compared with 0.934\\% for high-loss deletion and 0.390\\% for random deletion. This aggregate hides important exceptions: high-loss deletion is more disruptive on Arena at the larger budgets. Fixed-mask group-weight resampling also gives opposite high-loss-relative orderings across the datasets. These are conditional perturbation results, not uncertainty for a reoptimized deletion procedure or evidence of a causal effect of evaluation design. The contribution is an auditable, setting-specific sensitivity assessment rather than a universal ranking attack or a guarantee of stability.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nPairwise judgments are a natural interface for evaluation: a voter chooses between responses, or a game produces a winner and a loser. A ranking model turns these local outcomes into global scores. Such scores are useful precisely because they reduce a complex comparison graph to a compact summary. That compression also makes it difficult to tell which observations sustain a displayed ordering. Sampling uncertainty, dependence between observations, and targeted changes to the observed sample need not affect that ordering in the same way.\n\nWe ask a deliberately finite-sample question: \\emph{how much does a fitted ranking change when a small, explicitly constrained subset of its observed comparisons is removed?} This is not an assessment of the true quality of a model or team. The reference is the ranking fitted before deletion, not an external ground-truth ordering. A large change indicates sensitivity of that fitted summary; a small change under a particular rule does not certify robustness to every equally sized subset.\n\nOur audit couples a local influence calculation with bounded exact-refit selection. For each possible challenger to the clean leader, influence values define an immutable ordering of rows. Deletions must be nested prefixes of that ordering. A screening calculation reduces the contenders considered by retained-data fits, while an independent evaluator checks the clean-fit certificate and refits each deleted dataset. This separation matters: an internally predicted rank reversal is not itself a measured ranking change.\n\nThe empirical scope is crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences and historical basketball outcomes, fitted with the same static Bradley--Terry objective. These settings provide contrasting sources of pairwise data without constituting a controlled intervention on evaluation design. We find greater aggregate disruption than the measured baselines, but the ordering of deletion rules depends on dataset and budget. Conditional resampling makes that dependence especially clear: the high-loss comparator is consistently more disruptive in the stored Arena resamples and less disruptive in the NBA resamples.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe contributions are therefore narrow and verifiable: a source-inspectable frozen-prefix audit with independent refitting; a budget-resolved comparison against high-loss and random deletion on contrasting real-data settings; and an uncertainty analysis that explicitly separates repeated random masks from fixed-mask data reweighting. We do not identify a globally most damaging deletion set, isolate the causal contribution of shortlist refitting, or attribute the cross-setting differences to crowdsourcing rather than other dataset properties.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=0.98\\textwidth]{figures/audit_overview.png}\n  \\caption{A common audit for contrasting pairwise-data settings. A regularized fit supplies clean influence orders; bounded contender selection uses retained-data refits without changing those orders. Independent refits measure disruption. The uncertainty branch reweights provenance groups with deletion masks fixed: it does not repeat contender selection.}\n  \\label{fig:overview}\n  % @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L145]]\n  % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L831]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Pairwise ranking.}\nProbabilistic ranking has classical roots in tournament likelihoods and comparative judgment \\citep{Zermelo1929,Thurstone1927}. Bradley--Terry modeling \\citep{Bradley1952} and the binary-comparison ranking problem \\citep{Ford1957} provide the immediate foundation. Generalized Bradley--Terry estimation and software support richer fitting procedures \\citep{Hunter2004,Turner2012}; explicit tie models \\citep{Davidson1970} and bias-reduced likelihood \\citep{Firth1993} are distinct from the half-win ties and quadratic penalty used here. Models over permutations \\citep{Plackett1975} address a related but different observation space. Rank Centrality and counting-based ranking study estimation and recovery from pairwise comparisons \\citep{Negahban2012,Shah2017}. Our endpoint instead measures changes relative to an already fitted ordering, using a normalized inversion count related to rank-correlation statistics \\citep{Kendall1938}.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and sample removal.}\nInfluence curves formalize local sensitivity \\citep{Hampel1974}, while observation diagnostics connect data perturbations to fitted parameters \\citep{Cook1977}. Influence functions have also been used to trace predictions to training observations \\citep{Koh2017}. The infinitesimal jackknife approximates weighted refitting \\citep{Giordano2019}, and finite-sample removal robustness explicitly asks whether dropping a small subset changes a conclusion \\citep{Broderick2020}. We use influence only to define and screen constrained deletion prefixes. Retained-data fitting evaluates those prefixes; it does not make the prefix family exhaustive or establish approximation-error bounds for our selection criterion.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation and conditional uncertainty.}\nChatbot Arena motivates the preference-ranking setting \\citep{Chiang2024}. Vote-rigging research studies deliberate manipulation of that platform \\citep{Min2025}; here the intervention is deletion from a fixed observed sample, not the collection of new strategic votes. Basketball records provide a second source of outcomes \\citep{NBAdata}, not another language-model benchmark. Bootstrap methods distinguish the statistic being resampled from its reported uncertainty \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997}; the Bayesian bootstrap is a different weighting construction \\citep{Rubin1981}. Clustered and crossed-data resampling require attention to dependence assumptions \\citep{Field2007,Owen2007}. Our multinomial provenance-group weights are a conditional diagnostic, not a validated population uncertainty model for all dependencies in either dataset.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\nLet $D=\\{(a_i,b_i,y_i,u_i)\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ contain observed comparisons of $m$ participants. Here $y_i\\in\\{0,\\tfrac12,1\\}$ is the outcome for endpoint $a_i$, and $u_i$ is a stable row key used only for deterministic tie resolution. Define $x_i=e_{a_i}-e_{b_i}$, margin $z_i=x_i^\\top\\theta$, and loss $\\ell_i(\\theta)=\\log(1+\\exp z_i)-y_i z_i$. The clean fit is\n\\begin{equation}\n \\widehat\\theta=\\arg\\min_{\\mathbf{1}^\\top\\theta=0}\n \\left\\{L_D(\\theta)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i\\in D}\\ell_i(\\theta)\n             +\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2\\right\\},\\qquad \\lambda=0.001.\n \\label{eq:objective}\n\\end{equation}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L29]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L653]]\nHalf-win outcomes use the same logistic cross-entropy, rather than an additional tie-probability parameter. A retained subset is fitted using its \\emph{own} mean loss and the same $\\lambda$. Consequently, deletion changes the likelihood contribution relative to the penalty; it is not equivalent to keeping a fixed sum-loss penalty.\n\nLet $\\pi(\\theta)$ rank scores in descending order, with rounded-score ties broken by participant identity. For clean leader $l$, the primary endpoint is normalized pairwise inversion:\n\\begin{equation}\n I(D,R)=\\frac{1}{\\binom m2}\\sum_{a<b}\n \\mathbf{1}\\!\\left\\{a,b\\text{ have opposite orders in }\n       \\pi(\\widehat\\theta_D),\\pi(\\widehat\\theta_{D\\setminus R})\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:inversions}\n\\end{equation}\nThe secondary endpoint is the clean leader's new zero-based rank divided by $m-1$. Both describe fitted-ranking disruption; neither evaluates predictive accuracy. Budgets are $k_f=\\lfloor fn\\rfloor$ for $f\\in\\{0.001,0.005,0.01\\}$. Equal row fractions provide a common sample-mass scale, but do not equalize participant exposure, graph topology, information loss, or difficulty across datasets.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L680]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases]]\nPackage: draftwatermark 2024/03/04 3.3 Put a gray textual watermark on document\n pages\n\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/kvoptions/kvoptions.sty\nPackage: kvoptions 2022-06-15 v3.15 Key value format for package options (HO)\n\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/keyval.sty\nPackage: keyval 2022/05/29 v1.15 key=value parser (DPC)\n\\KV@toks@=\\toks17\n)\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/generic/ltxcmds/ltxcmds.sty\nPackage: ltxcmds 2023-12-04 v1.26 LaTeX kernel commands for general use (HO)\n)\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/kvsetkeys/kvsetkeys.sty\nPackage: kvsetkeys 2022-10-05 v1.19 Key value parser (HO)\n))\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/graphicx.sty\nPackage: graphicx 2021/09/16 v1.2d Enhanced LaTeX Graphics (DPC,SPQR)\n\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/graphics.sty\nPackage: graphics 2024/08/06 v1.4g Standard LaTeX Graphics (DPC,SPQR)\n\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics/trig.sty\nPackage: trig 2023/12/02 v1.11 sin cos tan (DPC)\n)\n(/usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/tex/latex/graphics-cfg/graphics.cfg\nFile: graphics.cfg 2016/06/04 v1.11 sample graphics configuration\n)\nPackage graphics Info: Driver file: pdftex.def on input line 106.\n)\n\\Gin@req@height=\\dimen142\n\\Gin@req@width=\\dimen143\n))\n\\watermarkTextWidth=\\skip51\n\n\n! LaTeX Error: Command \\watermarkTextWidth already defined.\n               Or name \\end... illegal, see p.192 of the manual.\n\nSee the LaTeX manual or LaTeX Companion for explanation.\nType  H <return>  for immediate help.\n ...                                              \n                                                  \nl.20 \\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n                                     \nHere is how much of TeX's memory you used:\n 1807 strings out of 475178\n 30470 string characters out of 5766538\n 416209 words of memory out of 5000000\n 24836 multiletter control sequences out of 15000+600000\n 558837 words of font info for 36 fonts, out of 8000000 for 9000\n 14 hyphenation exceptions out of 8191\n 72i,0n,79p,237b,36s stack positions out of 10000i,1000n,20000p,200000b,200000s\n\n!  ==> Fatal error occurred, no output PDF file produced!\nevidence_audit\nfinal_claim_validation.log\nfinal_compile.log\nfinal_scientific_evidence.log\nfinal_tex_validation.log\nreference_query_plan\nvalidation\n/usr/bin/pdflatex\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
